WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.139
Over the past two decades, K-12 school
reform has been dominated by testing,

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:14.250
standards, and charter schooling. Those
have been the fault lines in the

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.200
education reform wars. Well, we've
come to a pivot point.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:24.160
The new center of gravity? Mindset and race.
This shift was made explicit in my annual

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.060
education scholar rankings, which this year made
clear a changing of the guard — not just in policy

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.960
and public opinion, but even in terms
of who gets hailed as an expert

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.579
in terms of book sales, media
coverage, and more. The shift is not from

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.240
advocates to critics of yesterday's
reforms. It's a matter of a shift away

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.800
from one set of reforms and towards a
new set of priorities. Education's big shift

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.860
is not just about new legislation,
political agendas, and public opinion,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.100
but it extends to who gets celebrated as
an expert. That has big implications for

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.750
what research gets conducted and which
ideas are likely to shape the national

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.460
discourse in the decade to come.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.180
What do you think is the most promising
reform to improve American schooling?

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.560
Let us know in our poll. Also let us know 
what other topics

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.200
you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds and be sure to LIKE

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:23.119
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

